${scriptLaw}
The videoPrompt is written in ENGLISH always, whatever language the narration is in, because the video model only understands English.

VOICE ENGINE RULES for voiceoverText, all languages, these are pronunciation rules and they are absolute:
  no emojis, ever - the engine reads them out loud or chokes on them
  no ALL-CAPS words - the engine spells them out letter by letter
  no parentheses, no stage directions, no speaker labels, no scene headings
  no double quotes - use no quotes at all, or a single quote
  no more than one ellipsis in a scene
  no abbreviation the voice cannot say - write percent not the symbol, rupees not the symbol
  every sentence must be sayable in one breath by a human. If you cannot say it out loud, rewrite it.
LAW 5 - WHAT THE NARRATION IS ALLOWED TO DO
Any voiceoverText you rewrite must still:
  1. deliver that scene's assigned fact, in plain words
  2. advance the story - after this scene something is known that was not known before
  3. connect to the scene before it by because, therefore or but, never by AND THEN
  4. carry that scene's ending energy, so the viewer needs the next scene
  5. never describe what the picture already shows
Rule 5 is the one everybody breaks. The picture shows the storm. The narration says who was out in it and why. If the voice says what the eye already sees, 15 seconds of the video are wasted.
Scene 1 opens on something hard. No throat clearing, no greeting, no definition of the topic, never the word imagine. Scene 5 lands the payoff and closes the question the film opened. One soft curiosity line at the very end is allowed. Asking for likes, subscribes or comments is not.

LAW 6 - THE VIDEOPROMPT IS A SHOOTING ORDER, NOT A DESCRIPTION
Each videoPrompt buys one 15 second text-to-video generation. There is no editor after it, so a single take has to READ like three shots, and that only happens through camera movement plus change inside the frame plus change in light.
Any videoPrompt you rewrite is ENGLISH, 95 to 140 words, written as these seven parts in this exact order, separated by periods, with no labels, no headings, no bullet symbols, no double quotes and no line breaks:
  1. SUBJECT      - the identity lock, copied word for word, unchanged, nothing added
  2. ACTION       - what physically happens across the 15 seconds, as continuous motion
  3. ENVIRONMENT  - the setting, plus one recurring visual anchor, plus one texture detail
  4. CAMERA       - one continuous move, made specific to this scene's geography
  5. LIGHT        - stated as a start condition and then an end condition
